              },
              "scoreBarLabel": "You got :num out of :total points",
              "a11yCheck": "Check the answers. The responses will be marked as correct, incorrect, or unanswered.",
              "a11yShowSolution": "Show the solution. The task will be marked with its correct solution.",
              "a11yRetry": "Retry the task. Reset all responses and start the task over again.",
              "textField": "Please drag and drop the modes of administration into the appropriate slot:  *Oral* administration is the slowest absorption in this list followed by *Sublingual* administration, and then *Inhalation* administration and finally *Intravenous* administration which is the fastest mode in the list."
            },
            "library": "H5P.DragText 1.10",
            "metadata": {
              "contentType": "Drag the Words",
              "license": "U",
              "title": "Untitled Drag the Words"
            },
            "subContentId": "687e2cd7-272a-4b52-9a7f-31733066227c"
          },
          {
            "params": {
              "media": {
                "disableImageZooming": false
              },
              "answers": [
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div><strong>Cmax</strong> or <strong>concentration max</strong> is the maximum (or peak) serum concentration that a drug achieves&nbsp;</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>A mode of administration that achieves a very high c-max or concentration max is going to result in the drug being metabolised faster in the initial stage of metabolism.&nbsp;</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>There is a greater potential for overdose, side effects and complications with the mode of administration that gets the medication into the system the fastest.&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>No, this statement is incorrect.&nbsp;When a drug is in high concentration, it is MORE likely to bind to receptors and enzymes that it is not supposed to bind to. That binding is responsible for many of the side effects of the medication. <strong>Therefore, a drug that has a high C-max will be binding to many different spots that it is not supposed to bind to, causing lots of side effects!</strong></div>\n",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "<div>Indeed,&nbsp;this statement is incorrect.&nbsp;When a drug is in high concentration, it is MORE likely to bind to receptors and enzymes that it is not supposed to bind to. That binding is responsible for many of the side effects of the medication.&nbsp;<strong>Therefore, a drug that has a high C-max will be binding to many different spots that it is not supposed to bind to, causing lots of side effects!</strong></div>\n",
                    "tip": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>When a drug is in high concentration, it is less likely to bind to receptors or enzymes that it is not supposed to bind to.&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>\n"
                }
              ],
